<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Sealand dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1732 BCE – 1460 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom of the southern marshes that broke away from Hammurabi's heirs and ruled the far south of Mesopotamia for nearly three centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1732 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilum-ma-ili">Ilum-ma-ili</a> rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>, and established control over the marshlands at the head of the Gulf, known as the Sealand. He survived Babylonian counter-attacks, and his successors held the old Sumerian heartland as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire</a> contracted.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Sealand kings left few monuments, and their capital is unknown, though recently published archives from the dynasty and excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-khaiber">Tell Khaiber</a> near Ur show an organised state collecting grain and dates. Kings such as Ishkibal, Peshgaldaramesh and Ayadaragalama took archaic Sumerian-style names, and scribes copied Sumerian literature and omens, suggesting a self-conscious cultural revival. At times the dynasty held <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nippur">Nippur</a> and Ur, and it probably controlled Gulf trade with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-sack-of-babylon">Hittite sack of Babylon</a> in 1595 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassites">Kassites</a> took the north. Around 1460 BCE the Kassite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulamburiash">Ulamburiash</a> conquered the Sealand, and its last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ea-gamil">Ea-gamil</a>, fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>. The south was then joined to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kassite-babylonia">Kassite Babylonia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1732 BCE – 1460 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:25.23%;width:5.41%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1732 BCE – 1460 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century-bce">15th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ilum-ma-ili, Gulkishar, Ea-gamil</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesopotamian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Akkadian, Sumerian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>First Sealand Dynasty, Sealand</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1596&amp;focus=sealand-dynasty" aria-label="Sealand dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5804 990 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sealand dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6143 1252h0M6130 1223h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6124 1246h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6124 1246h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iraq. <a href="/atlas?year=-1596&amp;focus=sealand-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
